                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and introduces a compatibility in the sense of a mixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+law"} distributive law
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entwining"} entwining
            (structure). Then the relative Hopf modules become a special case of so-called
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entwined+module"} entwined module
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BrzezinskiWisbauer2003"} Brzezinski, Wisbauer 2003
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Geometrically, relative Hopf modules are instances of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+object"} equivariant object
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Škoda2008"} Škoda 2008
            %a{:href => "#dAndequiv"} d’Andrea, Paris 2019
            and its MR review by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriella+B%C3%B6hm"} Gabriella Böhm
            for an excellent comparison.
          %p
            Furthermore, in the context of relative Hopf modules there is an analogue of the faithfully flat descent along
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schneider%27s+theorem"} Schneider's theorem
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
